You have hit the nail on the head. What you are experiencing is a classic architectural anti-pattern known as the **"God Object"** or **"God Class"**.
Over time, VoucherRequestServiceImpl has absorbed too many responsibilities: HTTP orchestration, caching, multithreading, JDBC batch processing, Oracle PL/SQL execution, workflow state management, and UI data formatting. This makes the file a nightmare for source control (merge conflicts) and incredibly daunting for new developers to read or unit test.
To fix this, we must apply the **Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)** and the **Facade Pattern**. We will break this massive service into smaller, highly focused sub-services.
Here is the exact architectural blueprint to refactor this service safely without breaking your existing VoucherPostingController.
### The Refactoring Blueprint (4 Sub-Services)
You should create four new tightly-scoped services. The main VoucherRequestServiceImpl will act purely as a "Facade" (a traffic cop) that receives the request from the controller and delegates it to the appropriate sub-service.
#### 1. VoucherAsyncExecutionService (The Heavy Lifter)
Move everything related to background threads, chunking, and database locks here.
 * **Responsibilities:** Parallel processing, bulk inserts, and PL/SQL procedure execution.
 * **Methods to move here:** executeAsyncBatchCreation, processChunkRaw, executeAsyncBatchProcessing, executeApprovalInTransaction, executeAsyncBatchCancellation.
#### 2. VoucherWorkflowEngineService (The State Machine)
Move all the logic that dictates *who* approves *what*, and *when*.
 * **Responsibilities:** Routing approvals/rejections, schedule validation, and workflow audit logging.
 * **Methods to move here:** processWorkflowAction, logWorkflowAction, updateBatchSchedule, validateScheduledTime.
#### 3. VoucherQueryService (The Read-Only Data Fetcher)
Move all the complex jdbcTemplate.queryForList() and queryForMap() methods here.
 * **Responsibilities:** Fetching data strictly for the React UI. By isolating read queries, you ensure UI changes never accidentally break core business logic.
 * **Methods to move here:** getPendingBatchesForChecker, getAllRequestsSummary, getBatchSummaryDetails, getWorkflowHistory, getWorkflowSteps, getActiveRoles.
#### 4. VoucherExportService (The File Generator)
 * **Responsibilities:** Using Apache POI to stream data into Excel files.
 * **Methods to move here:** generateBatchExcel.
### How to Implement This Safely (The Facade Pattern)
You do not need to rewrite your Controller. You keep VoucherRequestServiceImpl as the main entry point, but instead of doing the work itself, it injects the new sub-services.
Here is an example of what your newly refactored VoucherRequestServiceImpl will look like:
```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
@Slf4j
public class VoucherRequestServiceImpl implements VoucherRequestService {

    // Inject the new modular services
    private final VoucherAsyncExecutionService asyncExecutionService;
    private final VoucherWorkflowEngineService workflowEngineService;
    private final VoucherQueryService queryService;
    private final VoucherExportService exportService;

    // ... repository injections ...

    // ==========================================
    // DELEGATED METHOD EXAMPLES
    // ==========================================

    @Override
    public void processWorkflowAction(ProcessVoucherRequestDto dto, String executorId, Integer executorRole) {
        // The main service no longer holds 100 lines of logic. 
        // It simply delegates to the Workflow Engine.
        workflowEngineService.processAction(dto, executorId, executorRole);
    }

    @Override
    public Map<String, Object> getBatchSummaryDetails(String batchId) {
        // Read operations go straight to the Query Service
        return queryService.fetchBatchSummary(batchId);
    }

    @Override
    public byte[] generateBatchExcel(String batchId) {
        // File generation logic is hidden away in the Export Service
        return exportService.createExcelByteArray(batchId);
    }

    // ==========================================
    // CORE LOGIC KEPT IN MAIN SERVICE
    // ==========================================

    @Override
    @Transactional
    public List<JournalRequest> createBatchRequest(BatchPostingDto dto, String creatorId, Integer creatorRole) {
        // Keep primary orchestrations here. 
        // For example, saving the manual rows to the database...
        
        // ... then handing off the workflow routing to the engine:
        workflowEngineService.initializeWorkflow(batchId, dto.getVid(), creatorId, creatorRole);
        
        return savedEntities;
    }
}

```
### Why this is the ultimate solution:
 1. **Zero Controller Impact:** Your API endpoints remain exactly the same. The frontend UI will not notice a thing.
 2. **Eliminates Circular Dependencies:** Background async threads sometimes require looping back to update statuses. Putting async logic in a separate file prevents the dreaded BeanCurrentlyInCreationException where a service tries to inject itself.
 3. **Developer Velocity:** If a future developer gets a ticket saying "Fix a bug in the checker approval logic," they instantly know to open VoucherWorkflowEngineService.java. They don't have to scroll through 1,500 lines of unrelated Excel generation and JDBC batch inserting code.
